<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?><ns2:section xmlns:ns2="http://rest.cis.illinois.edu" id="65574" href="schedule/2017/spring/HIST/207/65574.xml"><parents><calendarYear id="2017" href="schedule/2017.xml">2017</calendarYear><term id="120171" href="schedule/2017/spring.xml">Spring 2017</term><subject id="HIST" href="schedule/2017/spring/HIST.xml">History</subject><course id="207" href="schedule/2017/spring/HIST/207.xml">Publishing the Past</course></parents><sectionNumber>A  </sectionNumber><statusCode>A</statusCode><sectionText>Title:  Publishing the Past Topic:  How were Historical documents--the letters, diaries, and other artifacts through which historians learn about the past--published in the print age?  How will they be published in the digital future?  'Publishing the Past,' a part of the History Department's Source Lab initiative, introduces students to the history of documentary editing, and invites them to imagine new models for its future.</sectionText><partOfTerm>1</partOfTerm><sectionStatusCode>A</sectionStatusCode><enrollmentStatus>UNKNOWN</enrollmentStatus><startDate>2017-01-17Z</startDate><endDate>2017-05-03Z</endDate><meetings><meeting id="0"><type code="LCD">Lecture-Discussion</type><start>02:00 PM</start><end>03:20 PM</end><daysOfTheWeek>TR     </daysOfTheWeek><roomNumber>327</roomNumber><buildingName>Gregory Hall</buildingName><instructors><instructor lastName="Randolph" firstName="J">Randolph, J</instructor></instructors></meeting></meetings></ns2:section>